\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil stocks,  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  end lower on rate fears  \b0\par\par\uc2 320 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2005\par 16:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Brazil, Oct 27 (Reuters) - Brazil's stock market followed Wall Street lower on Thursday, while the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped on renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars and fears of rising U.S. interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 The Sao Paulo Stock Exchange's benchmark Bovespa <.BVSP> index lost 2 percent to 29,132, dragged down by steel maker Companhia Siderurgica de Tubarao.  \par \par \uc2 Shares in CST , as the company is known, fell 4.58 percent to 135.50 reais after it reported a 52 percent drop in third-quarter earnings.  \par \par \uc2 In the telecoms sector, market bellwether Tele Norte Leste Participacoes, or Telemar , slipped 2.14 percent to 38.86 reais even though the company said its profit nearly doubled in the third quarter.  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real <BRBY>, meanwhile, lost 0.53 percent to 2.291 to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "There's a question about rising interest rates abroad," said Daniel Szikszay, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Banco Schahin in Sao Paulo. Faster inflation could prompt the Federal Reserve to raise interest rates more, luring capital away from emerging markets like Brazil.  \par \par \uc2 The real was also pressured as \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the 17th time this month to bolster hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also pleased exporters by helped stop the real from strengthening further.  \par \par \uc2 Investors were digesting the minutes released on Thursday of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest monetary policy meeting, where it lowered its benchmark interest rate to 19 percent from 19.5 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The bank justified the decision by saying that the inflation outlook was "benign," suggesting that more rate cuts are likely in the coming months. That, in turn, could help speed up economic growth in Latin America's largest country. (Additional reporting by Nathalia Ferreira and Juliana Siqueira)  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-BRAZIL (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051027e1ar001ym\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
