\par\par \b \uc2 NY coffee slides nearly 2 pct on producer selling  \b0\par\par\uc2 325 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2005\par 14:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 26 (Reuters) - U.S. coffee futures sagged nearly 2 percent to a 9-day low Wednesday, pressured by selling from Brazilian producers and speculators reacting to technical price signals, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The New York Board of Trade's active December arabica contract <KCZ5> fell 2.0 cents, or 1.9 percent, to settle at $1.0170 a lb after trading from $1.01 to $1.05.  \par \par \uc2 March <KCH6> slid 1.80 cent to end at $1.0495 a lb, while back month contracts lost 1.10 to 1.75 cents a lb.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said stop-loss orders were triggered when the December contract fell below $1.0250, but met strong support at the low of $1.01, which was the bottom trade on Oct. 17.  \par \par \uc2 "We saw some technical selling and also Brazil has some good selling," said a trader, adding that producers in the world's No. 1 coffee growing country were taking advantage of a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hedge with dollar-denominated arabicas.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real <BRBY>, slipped against the dollar amid renewed worries that rising interest rates in the United States could lure capital away from emerging markets.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  15 times this month to buy dollars to help build up foreign reserves.  \par \par \uc2 At 1:00 p.m. EDT (1700 GMT), benchmark dollar-denominated arabica futures in Sao Paulo <ICFZ5> were trading down 1.8 percent.  \par \par \uc2 NYBOT coffee futures trading volume reached an estimated 14,188 lots, above Tuesday's official tally of 9,651 contracts.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Sara Lee Corp. on Wednesday said it has agreed to sell its U.S. retail coffee business to Italy's Segafredo Zanetti Group for $82.5 million in cash, as the company focuses on its meat, household products and baked goods \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  [nN26328409].  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-COFFEE (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|C|SOF|GRO|MTL  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051026e1aq001bx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
