\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Soy - Limited old-crop sales as CW planting starts  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Reese Ewing  \par \uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2005\par 18:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Brazil, Oct 21 (Reuters) - Brazil's soybean producers are selling small lots of the remaining old-crop stocks as planting for the 2005/06 (Oct/Sept) season starts slowly in the center-west, traders and analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 "There was some light business this week through Thursday but things are getting off to a slow start on Friday," soy trader Steve Cachia at Cerealpar said. "No sign of new-crop sales yet but with planting under way, forward sales should start soon."  \par \par \uc2 Cachia said 60-kg bags of beans sold at 32 reais on Thursday in Paranagua.  \par \par \uc2 Spot export premiums at the main grain port of Paranagua fell sharply this week to 55/65 cents (buy/sell) over front month Chicago Board of Trade futures, compared with 60/80 cents over a week ago.  \par \par \uc2 March new-crop premiums fell only slightly to 22/28 cents over from 25/30 cents above Chicago last week. April-May premiums fell to 5/10 cents from 11/15 cents last week.  \par \par \uc2 Jaquelini Maria Alves at analyst Multisafras Consultoria in Mato Grosso, Brazil's No. 1 soybean state, said early planting was under way and progressing well, overall, but some regions were still waiting for better rains.  \par \par \uc2 "Sorriso hasn't had sufficient rains yet to begin widespread planting but this isn't unusual. Rains in Mato Grosso pick up in the middle of October and wide-scale planting follows in this period, too," she said.  \par \par \uc2 Multisafras estimated about 8 percent of Mato Grosso's soy crop had been planted but said that the state's total planted area to the oilseed would fall this year from last by at least 8 percent due to the lack of producer resources.  \par \par \uc2 "There are still about 500,000 to 700,000 tonnes of old-crop beans in stocks around the state yet to be sold," Alves said. "Business is very quiet right now. Producers are focusing on planting."  \par \par \uc2 A bag of beans was selling at 24.5 reais in Rondonopolis, Mato Grosso this week and at 22 reais in Lucas do Rio Verde. "Prices just aren't attractive enough to move producers to sell right now," she said.  \par \par \uc2 Somar meteorologist Nadja Marinho said Mato Grosso has been receiving irregular showers over the past few days, principally in the southwest, with drier conditions in the east and some regions in the north of the state.  \par \par \uc2 "There will continue to be isolated rains next week. More generalized rains typically start about this time in the soy regions of the state, so we are a bit behind schedule -- nothing out of the norm though," Marinho said.  \par \par \uc2 In a foreign reserve building effort, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  several times since early last week to buy the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the real has weakened slightly against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 But the real was still trading at around 2.261 to the dollar on Friday, firmer than the 2.269 reais on Oct. 5 and around 2.90 reais a year ago.  \par \par \uc2 Following is a domestic price table for Oct. 20 from grain centers in key soybean states. Prices are in reais.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STATE GRAIN         Soybeans      Meal          Oil\par CENTERS            /60-kg bag    /tonne        /tonne\par                           (47 pct protein)\par -----------------------------------------------------------\par Barreiras (BA)      22.00        430              1350\par Rio Verde (GO)      25.50        420              1300\par Cuiaba (MT)         24.00        410              1100\par Rondonopolis (MT)   25.00     