\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil markets end mixed after volatile trading  \b0\par\par\uc2 566 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2005\par 18:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with markets close)  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Brazil, Oct 14 (Reuters) - Strong dollar inflows helped \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthen on Friday despite another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but stocks weakened as investors mulled a batch of mixed U.S. economic data.  \par \par \uc2 The Bovespa index <.BVSP> of the Sao Paulo Stock Exchange slipped 0.37 percent to 29,770 points, losing the 30,000 points level it had reached on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 But the Brazilian real <BRBY> strengthened 0.58 percent to 2.245 reais, supported by dollar inflows from exports, traders said, even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars for the eighth time this month to boost the country's foreign reserves.  \par \par \uc2 Trading in both markets was hectic and asset prices seesawed between positive and negative territories with investors prone to speculation.  \par \par \uc2 Early in the morning, news that the September U.S. Consumer Price Index (CPI) excluding food and energy came in below market expectations boosted Brazilian markets, leading the Bovespa index to rise more than 1 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The core CPI rose 0.1 percent against forecasts of a 0.2 percent increase, raising some hopes U.S. interest rates may stop going up sooner than expected, what could boost the lure of riskier assets in emerging markets like Brazil.  \par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the full CPI jumped 1.2 percent, much higher than the 0.9 percent expected by economists, and U.S. consumer confidence unexpectedly dropped in the first reading of October, putting some pressure on the market.  \par \par \uc2 News that U.S. commodities brokerage Refco Inc. began shutting down its main business amid a scandal involving its former chief executive also rattled investors. Fearing the collapse of the company may lead to a call of margins and a liquidity squeeze, some investors decided to sell riskier emerging markets assets to make cash, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "We had the CPI and markets reacted positively, but then we had all that confusion with Refco and markets fell again," said Alvaro Bandeira, director at Agora Senior brokerage in Rio de Janeiro, adding that the stock market is lacking a clear trend at the moment.  \par \par \uc2 Ademir Carvalho, a trader with Finabank brokerage in Sao Paulo, said stock investors are still pocketing recent gains.  \par \par \uc2 "The exchange is going through a period of profit-taking, but there is room for a rebound later.  \par \par \uc2 Shares for state-owned oil firm Petrobras fell 0.7 percent to 31.18 reais with the exchange's highest trading volume while miner Companhia Vale do Rio Doce (CVRD) gained 1 percent to 79.70 reais with the market's second highest turnover.  \par \par \uc2 Speculation that the departure of influential Mohamed El-Erian from Pacific Investment Management Company (PIMCO) could trigger a portfolio reshuffle at the fund manager, which holds about $28 billion in emerging markets, also pressured emerging bonds during the session, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 As bond prices fell and yields rose, risk spreads over comparable U.S. Treasury notes widened to more than 400 points for the first time since early September. At the end of the day, however, spreads retreated to end 3 basis points tighter at 388 points.  \par \par \uc2 El-Erian was appointed new investment chief at Harvard University, replacing Jack Meyer, who had run the university's $26 billion endowment for 15 years.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-BRAZIL (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M|RNP|DNP  \par \par 