\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Palocci: Govt Won't  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  In Exch Rate-Report  \b0\par\par\uc2 318 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2005\par 16:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister Antonio Palocci Thursday said the government won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop a rally in the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, the Agencia Estado newswires reported.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market are "not a good policy and never work," Palocci is quoted as saying.  \par \par \uc2 Auctions Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought an undisclosed amount of U.S. dollars were only aimed at increasing Brazil's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, he said.  \par \par \uc2 "The accumulation of reserves is necessary for Brazil to back up the stability of our economy," Palocci said.  \par \par \uc2 The real weakened to BRL2.282 per dollar at 1405 GMT Thursday, down from Wednesday's close of BRL2.268. The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, this year has gained more than 16% against the dollar. [ 10-06-05 1014ET ]  \par \par \uc2 Palocci also rejected any change in Brazil's current exchange rate policy.  \par \par \uc2 "We won't discuss our exchange rate policy again," Palocci is quoted as saying. "We think that the floating exchange rate (in place) since 1999 has served the Brazilian economy well."  \par \par \uc2 The recent strength of the real was caused by improving salaries, he added, while exports continue to develop well.  \par \par \uc2 Exporting companies have criticized government inactivity to stem the rise in the real, saying they are losing competitiveness.  \par \par \uc2 Exports, however, continue to boom. Brazil's foreign trade surplus in the year through September reached $32.67 billion, nearing 2004's full-year surplus of $34 billion. Analysts estimate the country will attain a trade surplus of $41 billion this year.  \par \par \uc2 Brazilian motor vehicle exports, however, fell 4.1 in September from the previous month. Carmakers have long complained that the current strength of the real renders their exports unprofitable.  \par \par \uc2 -By Bernd Radowitz, Dow Jones Newswires; (55 21) 3288 5004; bernd.radowitz@dowjones.com [ 10-06-05 1037ET ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020051006e1a60009o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
