\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  stocks plunge; real falls on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 433 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2005\par 16:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Oct 5 (Reuters) - Brazilian stocks tumbled on Wednesday after setting a record high the previous session while the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars for a third consecutive day.  \par \par \uc2 The Bovespa <.BVSP> index of the Sao Paulo Stock Exchange fell 3.58 percent to close at 30,163. It broke through 32,000 for the first time on Tuesday before investors started booking profits after heady gains that surpassed 12 percent in September alone.  \par \par \uc2 Most stocks slipped, with oil giant Petrobras off 5.35 percent to 32.75 reais on falling global oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 The broader selling was prompted in part by worries about potential U.S. interest rate hikes, which could lure capital away from emerging markets.  \par \par \uc2 "People are taking gains after several strong weeks and at the same time there are more worries about inflation in the U.S.," said Andre Querne of Maxima Asset Management in Rio de Janeiro. Faster inflation may prompt more aggressive rate hikes by the Federal Reserve.  \par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, the real <BRBY> weakened 0.40 percent to end at 2.269 against the dollar as the government bought dollars as part of a broader program to rebuild foreign reserves and try to attain investment grade status.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pleases exporters who are worried that their foreign sales may slow if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains near the four-year highs it has reached recently.  \par \par \uc2 Traders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may prevent the real, which has gained on heavy dollar inflows, from gaining beyond 2.20.  \par \par \uc2 "The real won't stay as strong as we had thought (because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), which was stronger than 2.20," said Tarcisio Rodrigues, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the Paulista brokerage.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  resumed buying dollars after a six-month hiatus on Monday and is expected to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  further in the days ahead as inflation, which normally accelerates when the real weakens, is firmly under control.  \par \par \uc2 Some say the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would need to be aggressive to absorb heavy dollar inflows from exports, overseas bond sales and yield-hungry foreign investors who are snapping up Brazilian assets to buy exposure to the country's lofty benchmark interest rate of 19.5 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Brazilian assets have rallied in recent months on sound economic fundamentals that have resisted a bribes scandal that weakened President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva's administration. (Additional reporting by Nathalia Ferreira and Juliana Siqueira)  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-BRAZIL (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051005e1a50019t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
