\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Opens Weaker Amid  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Expectations  \b0\par\par\uc2 297 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2005\par 15:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian real opened weaker against the U.S. dollar Tuesday amid expectations of a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market as a buyer of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The real opened at BRL2.239 per dollar, versus the Monday close of BRL2.230.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market Monday, buying small lots of dollars from two or three banks, traders said. Monday marked the first purchases of dollars by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was expected to step up its purchases partly as a way to brake the appreciation of the real, which has risen nearly 20% against the dollar so far in 2005. Putting a floor under the dollar would be a way to assure continued heavy exports in 2006, according to analysts.  \par \par \uc2 Another reason for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases of dollars would be to beef up foreign reserves. As of Thursday, foreign reserves stood at $57.04 billion, less than one year's imports of about $75 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Another factor that could support the dollar going forward is the prospect of higher U.S. interest rates, with many analysts predicting another hike in Fed rates in November. Higher U.S. interest rates typically cause a reduction in investment flows to risky emerging markets such as Brazil.  \par \par \uc2 Brazilian interest rates, on the other hand, are expected to decline, another factor tending to reduce investment inflows. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is due to meet again to review interest rates on Oct 19. Many analysts are predicting a cut of 50 basis points in the benchmark Selic rate, currently 19.5%.  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Murphy, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511-3145-1481; brazil@dowjones.com [ 04-10-05 1313GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1156 1843  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020051004e1a4000lf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
