\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Brazil Real Closes Slightly Weaker On Profit-Taking  \b0\par\par\uc2 314 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2005\par 22:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.    \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Sep 22, 2005 (ODC via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real closed slightly weaker against the U.S. dollar Thursday as profit-taking and importer purchases of dollars helped offset continued strong foreign-capital inflows, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 The real closed at BRL2.276 per dollar, slightly weaker from Wednesday's close of BRL2.275.  \par \par \uc2 Importers took advantage of the real's strength in early trading to purchase dollars at an attractive price, analysts said. Furthermore, investors locked in profits after the real's recent surge pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday to its highest level since April 10, 2002.  \par \par \uc2 Later Thursday, foreign-capital inflows helped offset the real's early weakness. Traders expect proceeds from several overseas debt issues to arrive in Brazil shortly. Several Brazilian companies, as well as the Brazilian government, have made large overseas debt offerings in the past two weeks, driven by demand from foreign investors for high-yield issues.  \par \par \uc2 The combination of importers' appetite for dollars and profit-taking in the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by foreign investors helped stem the dollar's fall and keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, traders said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bright economic picture, which includes tame inflation, falling interest rates and a growing economy, have made investing in the country and its companies more attractive and less risky in the eyes of investors.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil's risk rating as measured by the J.P. Morgan Emerging Markets Bond Index Plus was one basis point wider at 364 basis points over U.S. Treasurys as of 1940 GMT. That's one basis point off the tightest the rating has been since 1997. Brazil's benchmark 2040 bond was down 0.100 at 121.200.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jeff Fick, Dow Jones Newswires; (55 11) 3145-1481; brazil@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \par \par \uc2 09-22-05 1554ET  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020050923e19m000nt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
