\par\par \b \uc2 Strong Brazilian Real Leaves Execs Sleepless In Sao Paulo  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Murphy  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 963 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 April 2005\par 21:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--The appreciation of the Brazilian real is keeping some local executives awake at night.  \par \par \uc2 With the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a three-year high against the dollar, the head of the Brazilian unit of General Motors Corp. (GM), Ray Young, said this week in an interview with Sao Paulo daily Valor Futuro that he's in a state of "panic" and can't sleep because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement.  \par \par \uc2 GM, like many other companies here, is worried that the surprising continued strengthening of the real will cut into export growth. Though the country's trade accounts don't appear to be suffering yet, the government is keenly aware of concerns raised by big business and is considering steps to ensure that competitiveness is maintained.  \par \par \uc2 "Profit margins of all companies working with exports are falling," says Jose Augusto de Castro, vice president of the Brazilian Exporters Association, or AEB. "And it's getting worse."  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, the real was strengthening again Wednesday. At about BRL2.51 per dollar, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at its strongest point since May 2002 and a far cry from the now-unthinkable level of BRL3.21 registered last May.  \par \par \uc2 In practical terms, what it means is that exporters are taking in about 20% less in revenues than they did a year ago while simultaneously dealing with the production-cost increases implicit in Brazil's 7.6% annual inflation rate. Eventually, according to Castro, the cost-price squeeze is going to drive some companies out of exports and others out of business.  \par \par \uc2 Fearful of the negative impact the real's strength is having, Trade and Development Minister Luiz Fernando Furlan earlier this week said the government is studying ways to "compensate" exporters. Without providing details, Furlan said that the government will take steps to reduce red tape and improve transportation logistics.  \par \par \uc2 Furlan, former chief executive of food company Sadia SA, itself a major exporter, has been at odds with others in the administration on the issue of the real's appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 In November, when the real began strengthening in earnest, Furlan admitted that the trend was "a worry" and would likely hurt exports. He was immediately castigated by leaders of the governing Workers' Party for a lack of "team spirit." Asked about the strong real at a news conference in January, Furlan rolled his eyes, saying, "I guess we have to learn to live with it."  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Exports Growth Continues, But Data Spark Concerns \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Furlan's warning about weaker exports hasn't come to pass. First quarter exports of $24.451 billion were up 28% from the same period the previous year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indeed, booming exports are the main reason that the real has appreciated far beyond levels that had been expected by market participants.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The export dollars keep pouring in," said Raquel Fleury, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market analyst at the Tendencias Consulting Group. "Demand is robust among Brazil's trading partners. Meanwhile, Brazilian exporters have shown skill in winning market share."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The exporter association's Castro, however, says that first quarter trade number indicated a subtle threat. Imports of $16.13 billion showed a slower rise, about 23%, than did exports during the first quarter of the year, he noted.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It should be the other way around," said Castro. "With a stronger real, imports should be rising faster than exports."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What the lag in imports shows is that Brazilian consumers are not biting the lure represented by cheaper foreign products.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The rise in imports is temporary and comes from an increase in easy terms for consumer credit," said Castro. "It's all non-durable goods and foodstuffs. People are not buying big-ticket imports. The rise in imports will not endure."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The danger, said Castro, is that Brazilian consumers will stop buying domestic big-ticket items. At that point, Brazilian manufacturers will be losing money on exports but without the safety net represented by a robust domestic market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Said Castro, "Right now, exporters are shipping mainly in order to honor contracts and maintain market share."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That's exactly what Ray Young told Valor Economico. Exports accounted for a third of GM's Brazilian revenue in 2004. But exports are producing per-unit losses so far in 2005. "We may have to lose export contracts and reduce volume (if the real remains strong)," Young said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In some areas, Brazil is only retaining its edge because of the weakness of others.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The biggest problem for Argentina at present is a lack of competitiveness," said Cristiano Souza, an economist at the MCM Consulting Group in Sao Paulo, "Much of the Argentine industrial platform is outdated. Brazil has won an edge because of this."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meantime, the debate continues over how much further strengthening could be in store for the real, which has also benefited from sky-high interest rates - the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's key rate is at 19.5% per year - that have lured portfolio investments by overseas investors.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market participants say there's room for further appreciation, but say that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won't stay on the sidelines for long.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will set an effective ceiling on the appreciation," said a trader at a large Sao Paulo brokerage. "It could be BRL2.50 or it could be BRL2.40 or it could be something else, but there will be a limit."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As recently as last month, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bought dollars on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market daily, ostensibly to bolster foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves. However, analysts said that the bank may have also been testing the market reaction to its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tom Murphy, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511-3145-1478; \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 @dowjones.com; Rogerio Jelmayer contributed to this article [ 27-04-05 1925GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050427e14r0013n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
