\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Brazil Real Opens Slightly Weaker As Importers Buy Dlrs  \b0\par\par\uc2 311 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2005\par 22:05\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Apr 26, 2005 (ODC via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real opened just a tad weaker against the U.S. dollar on Monday as importers snapped up "cheap" U.S. dollars.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real opened at BRL2.524 per dollar, compared with   BRL2.523 at Monday's close.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monday's close represented the strongest value for the Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the U.S. dollar since May of 2002.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A "cheap" dollar led importers to buy the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the open on Tuesday, traders said. They said some banks and investment funds also were in a mood to buy dollars. The reason is the possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market by the Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  made heavy purchases of dollars in February and early March. The stated purpose of the effort was to build foreign reserves. But an effect of the purchases was to raise the dollar to BRL2.76 by mid-March. But the absence of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since mid-March has led to a steady appreciation of the Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be ready to reenter the market in order to prevent the real from strengthening beyond the psychologically important barrier of BRL2.50 per dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pressure for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been growing in recent days, with exporters saying the strong real could hurt Brazil's trade balance.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trade and Development Minister Luis Fernando Furlan on Monday responded indirectly to the concerns by saying the government was studying ways to "compensate" exporters for eventual losses caused by the strong real. Furlan declined to spell out what measures the government might adopt. Nor did he directly advocate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tom Murphy, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511-3145-1478; \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 @dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 04-26-05 0921ET  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020050428e14q000wx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
