\par\par \b \uc2 Latin America  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Diverge Vs Dollar In January  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Charles Roth  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 954 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2005\par 20:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Latin America's major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  turned in a mixed performance in the first month of 2005, marked especially by a divergence in the movements of Brazil's real and Mexico's peso.  \par \par \uc2 While Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been flying, Mexico's continues to sag. Elsewhere, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Argentina and Peru gained ground, and those in Colombia and especially Chile lost it.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil's real jumped 1.4% Monday to a two-a-year-high of BRL2.611. That brought the monthly advance to 1.6% and built upon a 9.4% gain in 2004. The real has strengthened despite routine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at propping up the falling U.S. dollar in Brazil's local market.  \par \par \uc2 But Brazil's COPOM \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  also wants to throttle inflation expectations, and has been aggressively driving up interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 "A more hawkish tone from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and inconsequential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has led USD-BRL to fall over 3% in the past 10 days," HSBC said in a report Monday. "While many - including ourselves - had thought the last interest rate hike would be the end of the tightening cycle, the Copom's latest minutes made clear this is not necessarily the case, and certainly any monetary loosening will be some time coming."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , "although this shouldn't prevent further BRL gains," HSBC said in a separate note.  \par \par \uc2 UBS Investment Research revised its projections for Brazil's economic growth, benchmark Selic interest rate and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate in a Monday report, raising its 2005 growth forecast to 5.3% from 4.3% and year-end Selic rate to 17.75% from 15%.  \par \par \uc2 It now sees the real at BRL2.80 by the end of December, rather than BRL3.00 thanks to the "consistent improvement in the external accounts and our current view regarding a much healthier balance of payments based on a strong capital account." Despite a recovery in domestic demand, Brazil keeps posting very large surpluses and the external accounts "continue to show an extraordinary adjustment," UBS noted.  \par \par \uc2 Although Mexico's peso has also been bolstered by monetary policy - the tenth tightening in a year came last Friday - it still weakened 0.3% against the dollar last month. In 2004, it lagged most other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the region, strengthening just 0.7% versus the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 On Monday, Standard & Poor's upgraded the country's sovereign debt one notch to "BBB," which contributed to the peso's advance to MXN11.1823 against the dollar. "While the upgrade and Friday's corto hike have helped lift the peso, a sustained MSN rally seems unlikely given an expected deterioration in the trade balance, reduced interest differentials with the U.S. and ongoing political concerns" ahead of 2006 elections, HSBC said in a Tuesday report.  \par \par \uc2 Guillermo Estebanez, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Banc of America Securities, said Mexico can't afford a strengthening in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to a "competitiveness problem: export growth in Mexico hasn't been as solid as in about any other exporting market."  \par \par \uc2 He also expects continued monetary tightening in Mexico, but thinks it will lag the U.S. Federal Reserve's rate rises. "So I don't think it (the peso) can strengthen that much," Estebanez added.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Mexico Governor Guillermo Ortiz seemed to hint Monday the tightening would top out at some point sooner than later, saying he expects core inflation will run around 3.8% for several months before starting to decline "more significantly" in the second half. The consumer price index should end the year within the targeted 2%-to-4% range, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Following Ortiz's comments, ABN Amro revised its year-end peso forecast to MXN11.80 from MXN12.00 a dollar, citing a report that the Bank of Mexico has set a floor for overnight interest rates, among "a number of other reasons."  \par \par \uc2 Ortiz also said that while oil prices may remain relatively high this year, he expects other commodities prices to fall.  \par \par \uc2 High commodities prices have been a boon to resource-rich Andean countries and Argentina.  \par \par \uc2 Argentina's peso ($1=ARS2.9225) climbed 1.8% against the dollar last month. Thanks to heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it was mostly stable against the dollar last year. Although authorities in Colombia and Peru also have tried to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from gaining on the dollar, both countries' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  advanced strongly in 2004, though Colombia's peso ($1=COP2363) shed 0.6% last month while Peru's sol ($1=PEN3.266) strengthened 0.4%.  \par \par \uc2 Chile's peso ($1=CLP580.20), though, has been captured by an interesting dynamic. After it climbed 6% against the dollar last year, it plummeted 4.1% against the greenback in January, including a 1.4% jump Monday.  \par \par \uc2 In a Friday report, Goldman Sachs said it expects Chile's peso to trade in a CLP570-to-CLP590 range for the remainder of the year in "an enduring tussle between supportive fundamentals and low-to-negative carry."  \par \par \uc2 Banc Of America's Estebanez said speculators have taken to borrowing pesos to go long Brazil's real given the huge differential between Chile's low and Brazil's sky-high interest rates. "It's shooting fish in a barrel," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "I'm sure the Chileans don't mind because it (the peso) was probably too strong anyway," Estebanez noted. He added that the peso-real carry trade helps Chile maintain its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export competitiveness while it enjoys both low inflation and solid economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 -By Charles Roth, Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2226; charles.roth@dowjones.com [ 01-02-05 1952GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050201e121000zk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
