\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Brazil Real Slips Early As Mkt Awaits Rate Hike, Eyes USD  \b0\par\par\uc2 321 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2005\par 21:05\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Jan 13, 2005 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 Brazil's real traded just a shade weaker early Thursday, as local traders looked to overseas \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for direction and awaited an expected domestic interest rate hike next week. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened at BRL2.703 to the dollar, compared with Wednesday's BRL2.701 close, and continued to hold near that level as the session got under way. The real has been trading at 2 1/2-year highs near the BRL2.7 level for almost two months as the U.S. dollar plunges against major world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and strong exports bring in a steady flow of dollars to the local market. Adding to the real's strength Thursday was the fact that a fifth straight monthly interest rate hike is widely expected from Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  next week. The rate hike, if it comes, will lift Brazil's base rate to a lucrative 18% per year or higher. Meanwhile, Moody's upgrade Wednesday of Brazil's credit rating outlook to positive added to perceptions that more dollars might continue flowing in. If the positive outlook turns to an actual credit upgrade, corporate and government borrowers might float more bonds and debt servicing costs would fall. But market participants mostly looked to the U.S. Thursday morning, where the release of fresh data on retail sales and unemployment claims could affect the dollar's trajectory. Traders were also on guard for further \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has entered the market with offers to buy dollars almost daily since the start of December. The dollar purchases have helped the bank build up reserves and also have discouraged bets that the real will continue to gain.  \par \par \uc2 -By Andrea Welsh, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511.3145.1481; andrea.welsh@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020050113e11d0049q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
