\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Opens Little Changed;Govt Denies To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 337 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Dezember 2004\par 21:04\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2004, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, Dec 22, 2004 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real opened almost unchanged Wednesday amid expectations among traders that the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, despite comments by the Finance Minister that the government won't wage a battle to keep the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from gaining too much against the U.S. dollar. The real opened at 2.703 against the dollar, falling slightly from Tuesday's close of BRL2.701, and then strengthened marginally to BRL2.698 in early morning trading. "The dollar is declining all over the world. We're not so naive as to think Brazil is going to affect the dollar's level worldwide," Finance Minister Antonio Palocci said in an interview with the Folha de S. Paulo newspaper published Wednesday. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is not an option." Nevertheless, traders said they expect even more aggressive dollar purchases by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that had bought an undisclosed amount of dollars in the market Tuesday. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have said such purchases are aimed at building the government's foreign reserves. Also Tuesday, investment funds and corporations bought dollars for purposes ranging from profit remission overseas to rebuilding of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. One Brazilian bank bought almost $100 million Tuesday, traders said. Multinational banks and manufacturing companies as well as international investment funds typically buy dollars at the end of the year to remit profits to parent companies overseas. The dollar has weakened considerably against the real in recent days. The real opened 2004 at BRL2.90 per dollar. In recent days it strengthened to the BRL2.70 level and above. Traders said companies were buying dollars for profit remission but also for early payment of dollar-denominated debts and, in the case of some banks, to build dollar reserves.  \par \par \uc2 -By Bernd Radowitz, Dow Jones Newswires; (55 21) 3288 5004; bernd.radowitz@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020041222e0cm0043e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
