\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Strengthens Despite  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 319 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2004\par 21:04\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2004, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, Dec 15, 2004 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real strengthened Wednesday although the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a fifth time this month \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars in the market. The real closed at 2.725 to the dollar, up from BRL2.765 per dollar at Tuesday's close. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought an estimated $100 million in the market at a rate of BRL2.754, traders said. But that wasn't enough to revert a strengthening trend of the real that rose after Brazil's lower house of Congress in a final vote late Tuesday approved an overhaul of the country's bankruptcy code. The government believes the measure could have a significant effect in reducing the country's high credit costs and increasing lending volume. \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this month came in an effort to avoid a too rapid valuation of the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that may hurt exporters. The real's strengthening came ahead of a decision by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its base interest rates later Wednesday. Most analysts expected the bank to raise the rate 50 basis points to 17.75%. Many investors hope that after a new hike Wednesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have ended its cycle of interest rate increases as inflation moves toward a government-set target. A weekly survey conducted by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  among more than 100 economists and analysts, predicted Brazil's IPCA consumer price index will rise 7.38% in 2004, and 5.78% in 2005. The government's inflation target for 2004 is 5.5% with a 2.5-percentage-point tolerance, while for 2005 it is 4.5%, also with a 2.5-percentage-point tolerance. -By Bernd Radowitz, Dow Jones Newswires; (55 21) 3288 5004; bernd.radowitz@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020041216e0cf00194\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
