\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Brazil's Real Steady Early As Mkt Eyes Econ Data, Ctrl Bk  \b0\par\par\uc2 441 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Dezember 2004\par 21:04\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2004, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Dec 09, 2004 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 Brazil's real held steady in early trade Thursday as investors eyed industrial output data and wondered whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the real as it did twice earlier this week. The real opened a shade weaker at 2.762 to the dollar, compared with Wednesday's BRL2.759 close, but then reversed course and firmed to BRL2.758 to the dollar as the session got underway. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants took in stride a Thursday morning report showing Brazil's industrial output slowed slightly in October from September even as it climbed 2.7% from October last year. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  started raising interest rates in September, but most economists don't expect the rate hikes to fully affect the economy before next year. Meanwhile, traders were on guard for further \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the institution stepped into the market to stock up on dollar reserves Monday after the real hit two-and-a-half year highs better than BRL2.70. The bank bought even more dollars Tuesday, leading \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders to conclude the purchases were meant to prop up the real. Caution hung over the market Thursday, even though the real has softened almost 2% since the start of this week and the bank stayed on the sidelines Wednesday. Trade Minister Luis Furlan has said lately he's worried the stronger real will slow an export boom that has revived Brazil's economy and lifted the trade surplus above $30 billion so far this year. Traders were also keenly attuned to a modest strengthening of the U.S. dollar against the euro Thursday morning. Most of Brazil's major exports are dollar-linked commodities, although Furlan has encouraged exporters to try to sell for euros if they can. Dollars are continuing to flow into the market, as Brazil exports keep bringing about $400 million per working day. Also supporting the real are expectations that banks will bring in more dollars by selling real-denominated debt to overseas investors eager to bet on emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Such sales have brought in more than $200 million in the past few weeks, and talk is circulating that the government will launch its own real-linked debt issue in coming months. Government-controlled retail bank Banco do Brasil (BBAS3.BR) became the latest bank to announce a real-denominated overseas bond issue Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 -By Andrea Welsh, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511.3145.1481; andrea.welsh@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020041210e0c9001c0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
