\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Steady Early As Mkt Eyes Econ Data, Ctrl Bk  \b0\par\par\uc2 435 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Dezember 2004\par 14:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--Brazil's real held steady in early trade Thursday as investors eyed industrial output data and wondered whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the real as it did twice earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 The real opened a shade weaker at 2.762 to the dollar, compared with Wednesday's BRL2.759 close, but then reversed course and firmed to BRL2.758 to the dollar as the session got underway.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants took in stride a Thursday morning report showing Brazil's industrial output slowed slightly in October from September even as it climbed 2.7% from October last year. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  started raising interest rates in September, but most economists don't expect the rate hikes to fully affect the economy before next year.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, traders were on guard for further \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the institution stepped into the market to stock up on dollar reserves Monday after the real hit two-and-a-half year highs better than BRL2.70. The bank bought even more dollars Tuesday, leading \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders to conclude the purchases were meant to prop up the real.  \par \par \uc2 Caution hung over the market Thursday, even though the real has softened almost 2% since the start of this week and the bank stayed on the sidelines Wednesday. Trade Minister Luis Furlan has said lately he's worried the stronger real will slow an export boom that has revived Brazil's economy and lifted the trade surplus above $30 billion so far this year.  \par \par \uc2 Traders were also keenly attuned to a modest strengthening of the U.S. dollar against the euro Thursday morning. Most of Brazil's major exports are dollar-linked commodities, although Furlan has encouraged exporters to try to sell for euros if they can.  \par \par \uc2 Dollars are continuing to flow into the market, as Brazil exports keep bringing about $400 million per working day. Also supporting the real are expectations that banks will bring in more dollars by selling real-denominated debt to overseas investors eager to bet on emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Such sales have brought in more than $200 million in the past few weeks, and talk is circulating that the government will launch its own real-linked debt issue in coming months. Government-controlled retail bank Banco do Brasil (BBAS3.BR) became the latest bank to announce a real-denominated overseas bond issue Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 -By Andrea Welsh, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511.3145.1481; andrea.welsh@dowjones.com [ 09-12-04 1313GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1156 1843  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041209e0c9000v6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
