\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Weaker After 2 Days Of Ctrl Bank  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 334 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 2004\par 21:04\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2004, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, Dec 08, 2004 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 The real closed weaker Wednesday as investors shied away from betting against the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased U.S. dollars in the exchange market in the two previous days. The real closed at 2.759 to the dollar, compared with BRL2.748 Tuesday. During intraday trading, the real fell to as low as BRL2.778 per dollar, but as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market Wednesday, exporting companies and banks started to offer dollars in the market again, strengthening the real before its close, traders said. Exporters in recent weeks had already flooded the market with dollars, driving the real up, as their revenues in dollars surged in synch with an export boom this year that has already pushed Brazil's trade surplus to a record $30.4 billion in the year to date. After the real had strengthened beyond the BRL2.7-barrier Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought a modest amount of dollars, purportedly to build foreign reserves. After the second and higher purchase on Tuesday, however, a consensus among analysts emerged that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was indeed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market. The dollar has plunged from BRL3.212 on May 20 to its current level, and Brazil's government is concerned that a stronger real could harm the current export boom. Renewed expectations about a real-denominated overseas bond issue by Brazil's government could put pressure on the real to strengthen again. The rumors re-emerged after government-controlled retail bank Banco do Brasil (BBAS3.BR) Tuesday became the latest bank to announce an overseas bond issue in the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Earlier this month, three Brazilian banks completed overseas bond issues denominated in the real. The bonds were equivalent to $225 million.  \par \par \uc2 -By Bernd Radowitz, Dow Jones Newswires; (55 21) 3288 5004; bernd.radowitz@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020041210e0c8000dc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
