\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Central Bank Intervenes \highlight\uc2  On  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Mkt, Weakens Real  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Murphy  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 902 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 2004\par 21:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--Amid growing concern that the strength of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could hamper the country's export competitiveness, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Monday for the first time since February.  \par \par \uc2 Brazilian officials have come under increased pressure the last two weeks to halt the steady rise of the Brazilian real against the U.S. dollar as a way to stimulate exports and assure a continued surplus in overseas accounts.  \par \par \uc2 Though the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  denied that it aims to weaken the real - saying instead that Monday's move was part of a strategy to build up foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves - some market participants said it was an indication that officials are uncomfortable with the current trend.  \par \par \uc2 "There has been a perception in the market over the last two or three sessions that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't want the real to strengthen beyond BRL2.70," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader. "Today (Monday) they brought out one of their weapons."  \par \par \uc2 Early in the session, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought what traders characterized as a a modest volume of U.S. dollars. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came at a critical moment in trading, as the Brazilian real strengthened beyond the BRL2.70-per-dollar barrier. The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars at BRL2.697. The real had opened Monday's session at BRL2.701 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 If the government's intention was to establish BRL2.70 as a ceiling for the real, then its first effort to do so was a success, traders admitted. The real hovered around the BRL2.72 mark during the rest of Monday's trading session.  \par \par \uc2 Over the long run, however, it is unlikely the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be able to enforce the BRL2.70 level, if that is what the bank seeks to accomplish.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not have the firepower to enforce a floor or a ceiling for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Darwin Dib, macroeconomic analyst for Unibanco, Brazil's third largest private bank.  \par \par \uc2 Said Ana Paula Rocha, an economist at ABN-Amro Bank in Sao Paulo, "The U.S. dollar is weakening all over the world. It's not the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is going to be able to go against the tide."  \par \par \uc2 "It looks to me like the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been looking for a good moment to buy dollars in order to build up foreign reserves," said Rocha. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been talking about doing this all year and they found a good opportunity to do it."  \par \par \uc2 Reserves stood at $50.4 billion as of Friday.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Exporters Worried About Strength Of Real \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But worries about exports and their relation to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate have surfaced in recent weeks as the real consistently gained ground against the dollar. The real opened the year at BRL2.90 per dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trade and Development Minister Luis Fernando Furlan gave voice to concerns about exports in remarks made only minutes before the actual \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Monday, saying, "the current level of the real scares exporters because they have had to absorb rising domestic costs this year in areas such as raw materials, public utilities rates and taxes, all of which cause their costs to rise."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indeed, even at Monday's close of BRL2.719 per dollar, the Brazilian real has gained more than 6% against the American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far in 2004, while annual inflation has hovered at around 7%. The combination is enough to put some exporters into the red, said Furlan.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such worries are well founded, according to the weekly survey of financial market analysts published every Monday by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . In this week's survey, the analysts forecast a 2005 foreign trade surplus of only $27 billion, down from this year's expected surplus of $33 billion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But does that matter?  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Not really, according to former \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  president Affonso Celso Pastore. "Brazil's macroeconomic numbers today would seem to support a rise in the value of the real," Pastore said, during a presentation at a Rio de Janeiro seminar. "Maybe exporters will suffer a little bit but the rest of society is doing quite well at the current exchange rate."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pastore argued that a $27 billion 2005 trade surplus was more than enough to assure another current account surplus for Brazil. Meanwhile, "a strong real will encourage investments in capital goods imports needed to boost Brazil's plant and equipment."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A strong real also helps counter domestic inflation by holding down the prices of imported goods, he said. In addition, it reduces the cost of paying off the dollar-indexed debts held by many Brazilian companies, especially in the public utilities area.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Henrique Meirelles himself attempted to dispel the idea that Monday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was aimed at setting some kind of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  band.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meirelles told the same Rio de Janeiro seminar, "We don't have any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate target. There is neither a ceiling nor a floor (for the real against the dollar)."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Said a trader, "Meirelles was a little vague, as he always is, but maybe this time he was telling it straight."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tom Murphy, Dow Jones Newswires; 5521-3288-5011; brazil@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Rogerio Jelmayer in Sao Paulo and Bernd Radowitz in Rio de Janeiro helped in preparation of this article.) [ 06-12-04 2044GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041206e0c6000w8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
