\par\par \b \uc2 Latin American news in brief at 2 p.m. EST.   \b0\par\par\uc2 473 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 2004\par 19:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Dec 6 (Reuters) - The following are top Latin American news briefs compiled from full-length stories that have run separately:   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real fell abruptly against the dollar Monday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced its first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to purchase dollars since February. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell as much as 0.7 percent against the dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  real strengthened in the morning to hit a 2-1/2-year peak of 2.699 to the dollar. The bank denied it sought to weaken the real at a time when exporters complain the strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was eroding their competitiveness. The bank said it acted to rebuild foreign reserves. The real trimmed its losses, trading down 0.37 percent at 2.719.   \par \par \uc2 -   \par \par \uc2 Brazil auto production jumps in 2004, strong 2005 seen   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Brazil - Brazil's auto manufacturers should boost their production by 5.4 percent to 2.3 million vehicles next year following a jump in output this year fueled by an economic rebound and strong exports, the sector's main trade group said Monday. Production in the first 11 months of this year was 2.02 million vehicles, 20.8 percent higher than a year earlier, with 1.4 million cars being sold domestically. Exports of motor vehicles rose 50 percent to $7.52 billion.   \par \par \uc2 -   \par \par \uc2 Peru workers strike to back U.S. smelter company demands   \par \par \uc2 LIMA, Peru - About 4,000 smelter workers launched a two-day stoppage Monday to support their employer, U.S.-owned Doe Run Peru, in a push to roll back a year-end government environmental deadline. Farmers in Peru's central Andes, where Doe Run's metals smelter is located, also blocked roads to pressure the government to agree to a five-year extension for the company to comply with toxic waste standards. Doe Run, an engine of the local economy, last month said it pull out of Peru if the government failed to give it more time to meet the ordered cut in toxic emissions. The company expects to export $510 million of refined minerals in 2004.   \par \par \uc2 -   \par \par \uc2 Cuba releases jailed reporter, 14th dissident freed   \par \par \uc2 HAVANA - The Cuban government freed an independent journalist Monday, the 14th member of a group of 75 jailed dissidents released on medical grounds as Havana seeks to repair relations with the European Union. Jorge Olivera Castillo, 43, was set free after serving 20 months of an 18-year prison sentence on charges of conspiring with the United States against Cuba. Castillo, who said he was suffering from chronic colitis and hypertension, had worked for independent news agency Habana Press, coordinated by a Cuban exile in Miami. The EU is pressing for the release of all 75 dissidents arrested in March 2003.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041206e0c600ako\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
