\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Real Closes At 2 1/2-Year High On Bond Sales  \b0\par\par\uc2 293 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2004\par 20:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian real closed stronger Friday, hitting a two-and-a-half-year high, on news of fresh overseas bond placements.  \par \par \uc2 The real closed at BRL2.709 per dollar, up from the Thursday close of BRL2.733.  \par \par \uc2 Three different Brazilian banks announced overseas bond sales through the trading day Friday. The bond placements will total $225 million.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Brazilian government announced plans to raise an additional $500 million by reopening an existing dollar-denominated global bond that was originally issued in July and which matures in 2014.  \par \par \uc2 The fresh inflows will add to Brazil's existing flood of export dollars coming into the market. Brazil has averaged about $600 million per week in its foreign trade surplus so far this year.  \par \par \uc2 News of the bond issues led to a strong rise in the real in mid-afternoon trading, with the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  briefly trading at better than BRL2.70. The real traded as high as BRL2.695 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar purchases by banks and importers pulled the real back across the BRL2.70 barrier by the close, however.  \par \par \uc2 The market was also reacting to comments late Thursday by \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Henrique Meirelles, who said, "Neither Brazil, nor any country in the world is in a condition to control international (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) flows and control the dollar."  \par \par \uc2 Market players took the comment as one more piece of evidence that \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to sustain the value of the U.S. dollar even though exporters have complained that a strong real hurts exports.  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Murphy, Dow Jones Newswires; 5521-3288-5011; brazil@dowjones.com [ 03-12-04 1941GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1156 1843  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041203e0c3000tn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
