\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Brazil Real Closes At 2 1/2-Year High On Bond Sales  \b0\par\par\uc2 299 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2004\par 21:04\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2004, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Dec 03, 2004 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real closed stronger Friday, hitting a two-and-a-half-year high, on news of fresh overseas bond placements. The real closed at BRL2.709 per dollar, up from the Thursday close of BRL2.733. Three different Brazilian banks announced overseas bond sales through the trading day Friday. The bond placements will total $225 million. Meanwhile, the Brazilian government announced plans to raise an additional $500 million by reopening an existing dollar-denominated global bond that was originally issued in July and which matures in 2014. The fresh inflows will add to Brazil's existing flood of export dollars coming into the market. Brazil has averaged about $600 million per week in its foreign trade surplus so far this year. News of the bond issues led to a strong rise in the real in mid-afternoon trading, with the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  briefly trading at better than BRL2.70. The real traded as high as BRL2.695 per dollar. Dollar purchases by banks and importers pulled the real back across the BRL2.70 barrier by the close, however. The market was also reacting to comments late Thursday by \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Henrique Meirelles, who said, "Neither Brazil, nor any country in the world is in a condition to control international (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) flows and control the dollar." Market players took the comment as one more piece of evidence that \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to sustain the value of the U.S. dollar even though exporters have complained that a strong real hurts exports. -By Tom Murphy, Dow Jones Newswires; 5521-3288-5011; brazil@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020041205e0c3000ou\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
