\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Brazil's Real Touches BRL2.70 Barrier Against Dollar  \b0\par\par\uc2 537 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2004\par 21:04\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2004, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, Dec 03, 2004 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real rose to the psychologically important barrier of BRL2.70 to the dollar Friday after comments by Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president indicating that \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't going to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a strengthening of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and amid growing investor demand for real-dominated bond issues outside the country. The real rose as high as BRL2.70 to the dollar in intraday trading, but then receded slightly to BRL2.706 at 1500 GMT. On Thursday, the real had closed at BRL2.731. The real hasn't traded stronger BRL2.7 to the dollar in two and a half years. "Neither Brazil nor any country in the world is in a condition to control international (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) flows and control the dollar," \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Henrique Meirelles said Friday. Meirelle's comments came just a day after Brazil's Industry and Trade Minister Luiz Fernando Furlan said the recent appreciation of the real was beginning to threaten the growth of the country's export sector, adding that the government hopes it could find a point of equilibrium that remains stimulating for the growth of exports. Brazil's real has surged from a low of BRL3.212 against the greenback on May 20 to its current level amid improving macroeconomic data and rising exports. The real also profited from news that two Brazilian banks on Friday completed separate overseas bond issues denominated in the real, worth the equivalent of $175 million. The interest of investors in real-denominated overseas bonds shows confidence in the stability of the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the country's economic fundamentals. Brazil's largest private bank, Banco Bradesco (BBD), said it raised $100 million from the sale of 36-month bonds yielding 17.5% a year. Uniao dos Bancos Brasileiros SA (UBB), or Unibanco, said it raised $75 million from the sale of 18-month bonds yielding 17.9%. A bank spokesman said Unibanco raised the initial offering by $25 million due to high demand. A third bank, Banco ABN Amro Real SA, is also seeking to raise $50 million from real-denominated bonds. Market participants have said they expect an increase in this kind of issue thanks to the strengthening of the real in recent weeks. Also, markets have speculated that the government could take advantage of the current strong levels of the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and also issue a real-denominated overseas bond soon. Export companies flush with dollars in recent days had flooded the market, contributing to the rally of the real. On Wednesday, Brazil reported a $2.08 billion trade surplus in November. While that was less than the average surplus of about $3 billion of recent months, it still pushed the year-to-date surplus to a record $30.2 billion, indicating that export dollars are likely to keep driving the real up. Falling international petroleum prices were good news for exporters as pressures on freight costs are likely to diminish.  \par \par \uc2 -By Bernd Radowitz, Dow Jones Newswires; 5521-3288-5004; bernd.radowitz@dowjones.com; (Rogerio Jelmayer in Sao Paulo contributed to this report.  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020041205e0c30007u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoa