\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's Real Opens Up As Ctrl Bank Rejects  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 329 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2004\par 21:04\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2004, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, Dec 03, 2004 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real opened stronger Friday after comments by Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president indicating that \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't going to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a strengthening of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and amid expectations of a possible real-dominated bond issue outside the country by Brazil's government. The real opened at BRL2.731 to the dollar, up from BRL2.733 at Thursday's close, and then quickly rose to BRL2.725 in early morning trading. "Neither Brazil nor any country in the world is in a condition to control international (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) flows and control the dollar," \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Henrique Mereilles said. Brazil's real has surged from a low of BRL3.212 against the greenback on May 20 to its current level of between BRL2.7 to BRL2.75 amid improving macroeconomic data and rising exports. Also, expectations the government could take advantage of the current strong levels of the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to issue a global bond or Eurobond denominated in reals, made investors hesitant to bet against the real. The government may wait for U.S. payroll data to be released later Friday before placing the real-denominated foreign bond in the market, traders said. Exporting companies flush with dollars in recent days had flooded the market, contributing to the rally of the dollar. On Wednesday, the government said Brazil reached a $2.08 billion trade surplus in November. While that was less than the average surplus of about $3 billion of recent months, it still pushed the year-to-date surplus to a record $30.2 billion, indicating that export dollars are likely to keep driving the real up. Falling international petroleum prices were good news for exporters as pressures on freight costs are likely to diminish.  \par \par \uc2 -By Bernd Radowitz, Dow Jones Newswires; 5521-3288-5004; bernd.radowitz@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020041204e0c3000w6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
