\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  eases early, stocks gain.   \b0\par\par\uc2 458 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2004\par 15:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Brazil, Dec 2 (Reuters) - Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased in early trade on Thursday after hitting its strongest level of the year in the previous session, while stocks added to historic gains as global oil prices slipped.   \par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real , weakened 0.37 percent early to 2.721 reais per U.S. dollar after finishing the previous session at its best level since June 19, 2002.   \par \par \uc2 "There is a little bit of pressure on the real because people think the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will say something to limit the euro's gains against the dollar," Davi Azevedo, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at the Treviso brokerage in Sao Paulo, said.   \par \par \uc2 ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet is speaking on Thursday. Traders in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  are waiting for signs of a potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  scheme.   \par \par \uc2 The main Bovespa index of the Sao Paulo Stock Exchange rose 0.15 percent to 25,273 points, extending gains that put it at its highest level ever on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 Bellwether Tele Norte Leste Participacoes (TNLP4.SA) was 0.36 percent higher at 42.10 reais.   \par \par \uc2 Oil prices retreated to their lowest levels since September, alleviating fears high petroleum costs would crimp global growth.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stocks rose in the previous session as Brazil's country risk rating, defined as the difference between yields on the country's sovereign bonds and U.S. Treasuries, fell to its lowest level since October 1997, when the Asia crisis rattled global markets.   \par \par \uc2 Falling country risk suggests foreign investors are increasingly betting on Brazil, along with other emerging markets, as the U.S. dollar tests new lows.   \par \par \uc2 Though traders warned that profit taking could start to take effect, markets have a very positive tone. Good news, like a rash of overseas bond sales, an improving current account surplus and a strong outlook for economic growth are undergirding buying.   \par \par \uc2 So far, markets have chosen to ignore bad news. On Wednesday, the monthly trade result for November showed imports hit an all-time high of $6.08 billion as a stronger real makes foreign made goods more affordable. If this trend continues, Brazil could see its trade surplus shrink.   \par \par \uc2 Some key government officials, like Trade Minister Luiz Furlan, have said the real should be weakened to around three per dollar to keep exports competitive.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week said the Treasury would gradually make $3 billion in purchases on the spot market through June, but traders said the effort will only stem pronounced appreciation of the real.   \par \par \uc2 "So far the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't acting heavily to buy dollars, they are going to wait until January. By then, inflation will be more under control, thanks in part to a strong real," Azevedo said.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041202e0c20084i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
