\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-Brazil to buy $3 bln in  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  market to pay debt.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Isabel Versiani   \par \uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2004\par 21:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, Brazil, Nov 24 (Reuters) - Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday said the country would buy $3 billion on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to pay down its foreign debt, a move seen by some as an attempt to halt the appreciation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The move eroded a rally in Brazil's real , which hit its strongest level of the year against the dollar shortly before the announcement.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed 0.55 percent weaker at 2.755 per dollar on the news, although economists were unsure whether the announced purchases were big enough to weaken the real.   \par \par \uc2 Fueled by Brazil's widening trade and current account surpluses, the real has strengthened for much of the last two years.   \par \par \uc2 But a pronounced rally in the real in recent weeks, sparked by surging dollar inflows and a deeper weakening of the greenback, has worried economists as Brazilian exports become more expensive and less competitive.   \par \par \uc2 President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva has also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was too strong.   \par \par \uc2 If left unchecked, some analysts fear a rally in the real could eventually cut the country's current account surplus. Brazil posted a surplus last year for the first time in a decade, helping cut its historically worrisome external financing gap.   \par \par \uc2 As of September, Brazil's public sector debt was pegged at an estimated $340 billion.   \par \par \uc2 NO \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Alfonso Bevilaqua, head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic policy department, said at a news conference that $646 million of the $3 billion will be used to pay down debt maturing this year with the rest used in the first half of 2005.   \par \par \uc2 Denying the decision amounted to an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bevilaqua stressed that \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that the government has no set target for the exchange rate.   \par \par \uc2 "The fact of this measure being announced implies a commitment with transparency but also that the economic policymakers were uncomfortable with the pace of appreciation of the real," said Mario Mesquita, chief economist at ABN Amro in Sao Paulo.   \par \par \uc2 "This does not imply the definition of a floor (for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), but discomfort with the speed of the dollar's decline," he added.   \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian Treasury will carry out the purchases over a period of time though traders said $500 million has already been purchased.   \par \par \uc2 Jorge Knauer, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Banco Prosper in Sao Paulo, said the Treasury had already been buying dollars in the market and traders had been aware of that.   \par \par \uc2 "This is not huge news for the market, but it is a way to act on the market's expectation," Knauer said. "When the dollar gets very low, analysts and political scientists begin to complain that the country can loose competitiveness abroad."   \par \par \uc2 In 2003, the Treasury bought more than $11 billion to pay down debt and rebuild the country's foreign reserves. Some of the purchases continued into early 2004.   \par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was floated early in 1999 after it was kept in a crawling peg against the dollar since 1994.   \par \par \uc2 Since its float in 1999, it slowly weakened to an all-time low of about four-per-dollar in 2002 on concerns that Lula, then a presidential candidate, would mismanage the economy.   \par \par \uc2 However, since Lula took office in January 2003 and impressed markets with budget cuts and a tough anti-inflation stance, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been on a gradual upward trend.   \par \par \uc2 Brazil is headed for a record trade surplus in 2004 with exports seen totaling $94 billion for a surplus of $32.5 billion. But the trade surplus is expected to narrow next year, partly as a result of the relatively strong real.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday raised its 2004 current account surplus estimate to $9.2 billion.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041124e0bo009i2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
