\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Brazil's Real Shows Life After Fed Hike  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mike Esterl  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,015 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 2004\par 22:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--So far for Brazil, the U.S. Federal Reserve's bark has proven worse than its bite.  \par \par \uc2 Two months ago, with the Fed poised to tighten monetary policy for the first time in four years, the real tumbled to its weakest level in 13 months as investors backed away from South America's most-liquid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Since the Fed hiked its benchmark lending rate a quarter percentage point to 1.25% at the end of June, though, the real has strengthened around 3% and was hovering at about three to the dollar on Monday - roughly where it was trading before Fed fears kicked in.  \par \par \uc2 "You're kind of back to where you were before the scare," said Guillermo Estebanez, a Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Bank of America in San Francisco.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil is a heavily indebted country and much of its debt is linked to the dollar or floating interest rates, making it particularly sensitive to monetary policy signals from Washington.  \par \par \uc2 But investors are finding a comfort zone again after the Fed signaled at its June 30 meeting that it plans to raise interest rates at a "measured" pace - and have grown even more comfortable in the wake of subsequent U.S. data showing a fall-off in job creation and benign inflation.  \par \par \uc2 That's helped bring back the so-called carry trade, whereby investors sell their dollars in order to invest in a country that offers higher bond yields.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil's debt was trading Monday at a spread of just under 590 basis points over U.S. Treasurys - narrower than 650 basis points at the end of June, but still providing plenty of room to make money. Brazilian bondholders had booked a 5.1% return in July as of Friday's close, according to J.P. Morgan's Emerging Markets Bond Index Plus.  \par \par \uc2 "In our view, Brazil is the most improved story over the past month," Credit Suisse First Boston's emerging markets research group wrote in a strategy note Friday, adding that two recent debt sales by the government had brought financing needs "to very manageable levels."  \par \par \uc2 While the Fed's go-slow approach has given the Brazilian real a lift, some local indicators - including an accelerating economy - are lending a further boost.  \par \par \uc2 Government officials are now predicting that gross domestic product will expand on the order of 4% this year, up from earlier talk of a 3.5% expansion and a healthy turnaround from last year's 0.2% contraction.  \par \par \uc2 "In our view, Brazil is one of the last remaining cyclical stories to be played out," Morgan Stanley's Latin American equities research group, which has an overweight recommendation on the country, wrote in a strategy note Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Much of this year's growth is being fueled by surging exports from the commodities-rich country. The last time the Fed began a tightening cycle, back in 1999, Brazil exported $48 billion. Local exporters expect that number to hit $90 billion this year.  \par \par \uc2 Economists are forecasting the country will book a $6 billion current account surplus this year - generated in large part by a $29 billion trade surplus, up from last year's record $24.83 billion windfall, according to a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey published Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Recent data, meanwhile, show inflation is receding as Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interrupts its rate-cut cycle. Monetary authorities in Brasilia haven't cut their benchmark lending rate, currently at 16%, since April and market participants don't expect to see a change when the monthly policy meeting ends on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Reflecting the improved sentiment, traders in New York and Sao Paulo swapped rumors last week of a possible credit ratings upgrade for the junk-rated country even as credit agencies suggested no quick move was in the cards.  \par \par \uc2 Most \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts say the real could log some more advances in the coming days and weeks - but probably just baby steps, given that \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the rally picks up too much steam.  \par \par \uc2 "The government's not going to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate much more from here, because they really need the weak real to lock in the trade surplus," said Lara Rhame, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought greenbacks and stopped rolling over dollar-denominated debt in 2003 and earlier this year, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  threatened to strengthen to less than BRL2.80 to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Momentum also could be capped by some residual concern that rising wholesale prices could feed into consumer prices, added Rhame, who reckons the real could strengthen to BRL2.95 to the dollar in the near term but will have a hard time reaching BRL2.90.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen as eager to boost its international reserves, particularly after Finance Minister Antonio Palocci said last month the government doesn't plan to renew a $14 billion loan program with the International Monetary Fund that expires at the end of the year.  \par \par \uc2 "Basically, we see the real as range-bound," said Marcelo Mesquita, ABN Amro's chief economist in Sao Paulo.  \par \par \uc2 Mesquita said he expects Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to end the year at BRL3.05, even though he plans to raise his economic growth forecast for 2004, currently at 3.7%, later this week on the heels of better-than-expected manufacturing data.  \par \par \uc2 But investors also will keep a wary eye on the Fed to make sure it doesn't bare its fangs any time soon.  \par \par \uc2 If the Fed discards its talk of "measured" rate hikes, analysts say, the real could quickly retreat back above BRL3.20, a level it reached in intraday trade on May 21, when market nervousness peaked.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (Mike Esterl, a former Mexico bureau chief and Latin America editor for Dow Jones Newswires, is a special writer in the emerging markets group.) \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Mike Esterl, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4026; mike.esterl@dowjones.com [ 19-07-04 2005GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040719e07j000sq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
