\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Opens Slightly Stronger At BRL2.874 Tuesday  \b0\par\par\uc2 348 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 15:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--Brazil's real opened slightly stronger against the U.S. dollar Tuesday with little news out on a continuing campaign finance scandal amid expectations of reports indicating that inflation continues to slow in South America's largest economy.  \par \par \uc2 The real traded at BRL2.874 for each dollar, compared with Monday's close of BRL2.876.  \par \par \uc2 The real has strengthened more than 4% over the last two weeks as President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva and his congressional allies have pushed to derail congressional inquiries into allegations of improper campaign contributions to his Worker's Party.  \par \par \uc2 A key measure of inflation in Brazil is slated to be released Tuesday afternoon but is unlikely to move the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market because economists and investors expect the report to show inflation slowing.  \par \par \uc2 If inflation indexes to be released Tuesday and later this week show that price hikes are easing, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may opt to cut its benchmark 16.25% interest rate later this month. An interest rate cut could spur a rally in the real.  \par \par \uc2 The government is expected to announce a spending package Tuesday that is supposed to help boost economic growth. However, the plan is likely to be a repackaging of existing spending programs with little in the way of new outlays. Analysts believe the package is more of a political move designed to distract attention from the simmering campaign finance scandal, which allegedly involved a former aide to Lula's Chief-of-Staff Jose Dirceu.  \par \par \uc2 Finally, there is speculation among traders that \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may move to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and buy dollars to keep the real from strengthening further this week. If the real continues to trade stronger than BRL2.90 reals per dollar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could buy dollars for the first time in weeks under an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy launched this year to rebuild \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Tom Vogel, Dow Jones Newswires; (5511) 3145-1480; \par tom.vogel@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 09-03-04 1423GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040309e039000m3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
