\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil says bought $2.6 bln on  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  market in Jan.   \b0\par\par\uc2 306 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Februar 2004\par 17:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, Brazil, Feb 18 (Reuters) - Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday it bought $2.6 billion on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in January to bolster international reserves.   \par \par \uc2 In early January, the bank changed its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and began snapping up dollars on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on a regular basis.   \par \par \uc2 But when the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  it only provides the exchange rate at which it buys greenbacks, declining to confirm the amount of each purchase.   \par \par \uc2 Previously, state-run Banco do Brasil (BBAS3.SA) would buy dollars on the Treasury's behalf but rarely confirmed its presence in the market.   \par \par \uc2 The Treasury bought $11.2 billion on the spot market in 2003, $6.3 billion of which came in the fourth quarter alone. In addition to bolstering reserves, the Treasury uses that money to make debt payments abroad.   \par \par \uc2 So far in February, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  once in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to buy dollars.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been struggling to beef up its international reserves after a series of financial crises in the late 1990s, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold billions of dollars as it sought to prop up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real .   \par \par \uc2 The country finished last year with net international reserves of just $17.37 billion, which excludes loan money from the International Monetary Fund. That's a far cry from the mid-1990s, when Brazil's net reserves surpassed $60 billion.   \par \par \uc2 Most economists expect the country to finish 2004 with net reserves of around $30 billion, which should help make Latin America's largest economy less vulnerable to external shocks.   \par \par \uc2 In addition to buying dollars on the spot market, Brazil is also expected to raise extra cash by issuing sovereign bonds abroad. In January, Brazil sold $1.5 billion in global bonds.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040218e02i003f3\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (brazil near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Datum\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/1995 bis 21/08/2023\cell \par